  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nThe hot and dry weather continues through this weekend and into \nthe first half of next week, but the overall conditions become a \nlittle more favorable Monday and Tuesday for streamer showers in \nthe morning and scattered afternoon sea-breeze thunderstorms.\n\nThe big talking point for next week remains the potential arrival\nof a \"cold\" front late Wednesday into Thursday. However, there\nremains significant uncertainty regarding the southern extent of \nthe front, the strength, and the timing.\n\nHurricane Polo continues to be a thorn in the side of the models\nwhen it comes to determining how it will interact with the parent\ntrough as it moves across the Desert Southwest early next week.\nThere has been a trend towards it having less of an impact on the\ndepth of the trough compared to what some of the models were\nshowing yesterday. \n\nThis means the trough would likely stay farther north, and thus a\nbetter chance of the front stalling over Hill Country and not \nmaking it into South Texas. But even if that is the case, a \npattern change is coming and that will open the door to more \nwesterly flow, increased moisture, and ultimately a better chance \nfor precipitation and cooler temperatures from the middle of next\nweek onward.",
